    description:
      "Work–family conflict is time-based, strain-based and behaviour-based, and its consequences for satisfaction, health and family functioning are well documented. Qualitative and person-centred research identifies boundary-work tactics — fixed stop times, a physical work zone, communicating expectations, batching email — and finds that fit between preferred and actual segmentation, not segmentation per se, predicts well-being. Experimental and diary work shows after-hours electronic communication raises anger and work–nonwork conflict, and that smartphone use in the evening blocks recovery. The boundary: what fits differs by job and person, and boundaries need a supportive supervisor to hold.",
    action: "Write three boundary rules this week — a fixed daily stop time, a place where work does not happen, and a rule that work email is not opened between the evening meal and the children's bedtime — tell your team and your partner, and review what leaked after 30 days.",
