    description:
      "Experience Corps places older volunteers in elementary schools for regular tutoring and mentoring. A pilot randomised trial found increased physical, cognitive and social activity in volunteers, and a later study found evidence of neurocognitive plasticity in at-risk older adults. Reviews of intergenerational programmes find consistent positive effects on children's attitudes toward older adults and variable but often beneficial effects on older participants' well-being, depression and self-esteem, and community-level work links sustained programmes to neighbourhood trust and social capital. The boundary: many studies are small and non-randomised, benefits are not systematic, and quality depends on organisation and staff training.",
    action: "Enrol an aging parent (or yourself, if retired) in a structured intergenerational program with a fixed weekly commitment — school tutoring, a shared-site care program or a community mentoring scheme — and review energy, mood and enjoyment after three months.",
